- Depot constraints: Each depot’s outbound shipments are limited by on-hand stocks and throughput for each supply type within the period.
- Demand fulfillment: Each supported unit has required quantities by supply type. Authorized shortfalls are permitted only as a last resort and will incur penalty costs to reflect operational risk from unmet demand.
- Vehicle loading: Convoy loads are constrained by vehicle capacity. Each supply type consumes capacity based on its volumetric equivalency; mixed loads are allowed provided total volume does not exceed available vehicle capacity.
- Route activation and security overhead: Movements occur along designated depot–unit routes. A route must be activated before any vehicles can move. Activating any route triggers a fixed Risk Cost that represents security overhead (route clearance, escort teams, overwatch, and command-and-control) and is incurred once per route per period regardless of the number of vehicles dispatched. No vehicles may use a route unless it is activated.
- Fleet limitation: The total number of vehicles dispatched across all routes cannot exceed the available fleet. Each vehicle deployed carries an operating cost; underutilized convoys should be avoided.
- Cost trade-offs: Transport costs vary by route and supply type, reflecting distance, terrain, and speed. The plan will balance transport costs, fixed route risk costs, per-vehicle operating costs, and shortage penalties. Consolidating flows onto fewer activated routes is preferred where feasible, but additional routes may be justified to avert high penalty costs or reduce exposure on a single corridor.
- Priorities and coordination: Priority is given to units in contact and critical mobility enablers. Depots will pre-stage loads, security forces will synchronize route clearance and escort timelines, and units will confirm demand and offload posture. If a route is interdicted, shipments will be rerouted via alternate activated corridors subject to fleet and capacity limits.

